---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Divider from '../components/Divider.astro';
import '../styles/landing.css';
---

<Layout title="Law Firm Technology Consulting" description="Wichita law firm technology consulting: IT issue tracking, discovery review tools, reporting dashboards, and website builds from an independent local consultant.">

<!-- ── PAGE HERO ── -->
<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-grid"></div>
  <div class="page-hero-glow"></div>
  <div class="page-hero-inner">
    <div class="page-eyebrow fade-up d1">
      <span class="eyebrow-num">// Legal Tech</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="page-headline fade-up d2">
      Law Firm<br>Technology Consulting<br><em>in Wichita</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="page-sub fade-up d3">
      Practical technology for small and mid-size law firms: IT tracking, evidence review
      tools, reporting dashboards, and websites. Built by someone who works inside a law firm.
    </p>
    <div class="page-hero-meta fade-up d4">
      <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-dot"></span>Wichita, KS</div>
      <div class="meta-divider"></div>
      <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-dot"></span>Independent Consultancy</div>
      <div class="meta-divider"></div>
      <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-dot"></span>Free Scoping Call</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── INDEPENDENT, AND WHO I AM ── -->
<div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">01</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Independent, and<br><em>Who I Am</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        DataForge is a separate, independent company, and I work as a law firm technology
        consultant here in Wichita. I use DataForge's tools in my work at a Wichita law firm,
        and this page speaks for DataForge, not for the firm.
      </p>
      <p>
        I started at that firm as an office clerk, and the firm later created a Data Coordinator
        role around the systems I'd built.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── THE PROBLEM ── -->
<div style="background: var(--bg2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">02</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">The<br><em>Problem</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        Many law firms don't have an in-house IT department. Printers jam, software rollouts
        go unexplained, and when something breaks the fix depends on who happens to be nearby.
        Each issue looks like a one-off because nobody keeps a record, so the underlying
        patterns stay invisible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── IT LOGGER ── -->
<div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">03</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Tracking IT Issues:<br><em>IT Logger</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        In my office, a vendor was called only for major failures and nothing tracked the
        smaller problems. I built IT Logger, a small ticket tracker (a log of who reported what,
        when, and whether it's fixed). It runs locally on any machine with Python, needs no
        licenses or outside services, and shows ticket counts, issue types, and monthly volume
        on a dashboard.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once issues were logged, a pattern appeared. Printer trouble was the biggest category
        and clustered around the same shared printers. The training questions clustered around
        a document-management software rollout that never had a structured handoff. Individual
        annoyances turned out to have fixable causes.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="lp-figure reveal">
      <img src="/images/tools/itlogger-dashboard.webp" alt="IT Logger dashboard showing total, open and resolved ticket counts and average resolution time, a donut chart of issue types, and a bar chart of monthly ticket volume over six months." width="1600" height="535" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      <figcaption>Dashboard with ticket totals, issue type distribution and monthly volume</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="lp-actions">
      <a href="/work/it-triage/" class="btn-ghost">Read the Write-Up: When Nobody Owns IT →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── CASELENS ── -->
<div style="background: var(--bg2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">04</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Reviewing Discovery Data:<br><em>CaseLens</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        Discovery is the stage where both sides hand over case data. CaseLens, built by
        DataForge, is a Windows desktop app for reviewing exported message data. A reviewer
        can browse threads, search by keyword, filter by sender or channel, and export findings
        to CSV (a plain spreadsheet file). Cases open read-only, so the source data can't be
        changed by accident.
      </p>
      <p>
        It started as a simple message browser and grew in stages as real needs came up.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── EVIDENCE PROCESSING SUITE ── -->
<div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">05</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Evidence Processing:<br><em>Evidence Suite</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        The Evidence Processing Suite, also built by DataForge, is a companion toolset for
        evidence files. It pulls still frames out of video, reads the text in images and
        screenshots (OCR, optical character recognition), and lays frames out in contact sheets
        (PDF grids of frames with timestamps) for quick attorney review.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also records a SHA-256 hash for each evidence file. A hash is a digital fingerprint
        that changes if a file is altered, so you can check later whether a file is unchanged.
        The results are packaged into structured PDFs. I make no claim about what any court
        will accept; that's a question for counsel.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── REPORTING AND DASHBOARDS ── -->
<div style="background: var(--bg2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">06</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Reporting and<br><em>Dashboards</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        Firms often can't see their own numbers without someone running a report by hand.
        I build dashboards (one screen of live charts and tables) and automated reports
        (reports that build themselves on a schedule) from the data you already have.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/dashboard-gallery/">dashboard gallery</a> includes a sample legal billing
        dashboard. It uses made-up data and is there to show what's possible, not to show any
        real firm's numbers.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── WEBSITE ── -->
<div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">07</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Your Firm's<br><em>Website</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal">
      <p>
        I also build websites for law firms: attorney profiles, practice-area pages, a blog,
        and a CMS (the editing area behind the site) that your own staff can update, with
        training and handoff documentation. See
        <a href="/small-business-website-design-wichita/">small business website design in Wichita</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── WORKING TOGETHER ── -->
<div style="background: var(--bg2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header reveal">
      <span class="section-num">08</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Working<br><em>Together</em></h2>
    </div>
    <Divider variant="circuit" />

    <div class="lp-prose reveal" style="margin-bottom:32px;">
      <p>
        Engagements start with a free scoping call. Pricing depends on the kind of work, and
        ongoing support is separate from building.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="lp-tiers reveal">
      <div class="lp-tier">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">Starter<span class="lp-tier-price">$500–$1,500 per project</span></div>
        <p>A single, scoped deliverable, such as a dashboard, an automation script, or a landing page.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-tier">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">Growth<span class="lp-tier-price">$1,500–$3,000 a month</span></div>
        <p>
          The monthly price while a larger build is actively underway. It's for building
          things, not for ongoing support.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-tier">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">Strategic<span class="lp-tier-price">Quoted</span></div>
        <p>Larger or multi-system work, scoped and priced after a discovery call.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lp-tier">
        <div class="lp-tier-name">Care plans<span class="lp-tier-price">$250 / $750 a month</span></div>
        <p>
          Ongoing support for tools and sites already in place: Care at $250 a month and
          Priority Care at $750 a month, each with included hours.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="lp-actions">
      <a href="/services/" class="btn-ghost">See Full Services &amp; Pricing →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── CTA STRIP ── -->
<div class="cta-strip">
  <div class="cta-glow"></div>
  <div class="cta-eyebrow">// Start Here</div>
  <h2 class="cta-headline">Let's Look at<br><em>Your Firm's Tech.</em></h2>
  <p class="cta-sub">
    Tell me about your firm's technology headaches. Every engagement starts with a free
    scoping call.
  </p>
  <div class="cta-actions">
    <a href="/contact/" class="btn-primary">Book a Free Scoping Call</a>
    <a href="/services/" class="btn-ghost">See Services &amp; Pricing</a>
  </div>
</div>

</Layout>
